From: levy@cs.utk.edu
Date: Fri, 9 Dec 1994 17:32:13 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-12-390] MEME: Inevitability/Gibson

> 
> Webb_S <WebbS@oplc.psb.bls.gov> writes:
> 
> This also makes me wonder how Extropians feel about the cyberpunk genre of 
> literature.  The only cyberpunk novel I've actually read is Gibson's 
> _Neuromancer_, but it seems pretty obvious that the world portrayed in 
> Gibson's books was faithfully duplicated in the Cyberpunk role-playing game. 

I think you need to take Gibson in context.  I see him as coming out of a long
tradition of North American writers who used a gloom-and-doom backdrop to create
a mood: esp. Raymond Chandler, William Burroughs, Thomas Pynchon, and Philipp 
K. Dick; cf. Gibson's reply to an interviewer's question about whether Gibson 
had read Dick: "I didn't have to; I read Pynchon", or something similar.  
For Chandler, the mood came from the seediness of the decadent L.A. underworld 
of the 30's; for Pynchon, it came from various other scenarios (e.g. the 
wartime setting of _Gravity's Rainbow_); for Burroughs (and Dick), it came from the author's own dependence on narcotics, and for Dick, from the strange 
insights he had about the issues surrounding notions like reality and identity.
The same threads of hip alienation run through the music of groups like
Steely Dan (named after a character in a Burroughs novel), and fans of
that group will recognize the Steely Dan references in Gibson's fiction.

In order to set up a mood, an author has to exaggerate enough to create
something different from what readers experience in their everyday lives.
Gibson latched on to the sexy world of VR and the possible implications
of privatization; he exaggerated them for effect, and wrote some of
the best literature I've ever read.  (I don't think I'll ever forget his 
scene of an AI constructing art out of the whirling flotsam of a family's
tragic history.) But I don't take his exaggerations as accurate predictions 
of what privatization would look like, any more than I take, say, the goofy 
world of "The Jetsons" as an accurate depiction of the average family 
a few centuries from now.

Gibson ran out of steam when he stopped using dystopia as a background for 
technology, and focused instead on the dystopia itself, as he did
in _Virtual Light_, which pales by comparison to _Neuromancer_.  Naturally
enough, the mainstream critics started taking him more seriously when
he entered this stage.

> 
> On a related note, I've been wondering lately whether were one's willingness 
> to embrace an anarcho-capitalist future (which look rather scary to me) has 
> anything to do with where one's from.  A recent _Technology Review_ article 
> attempted to explain the success of Silicon Valley compared to computer 
> companies in the East as (in part, anyway) the result of West Coast 
> flexibility vs. New England's desire for stability.  It seems to me that a 
> disproportionate number of 'Tropes are from the West Coast.  Is there a 
> parallel here?  Are bay area folks more risk-seeking and adaptable than New 
> Yorkers such as myself?  Personally, I still dream of living in the little 
> house in the suburbs with a wife, 2.3 children, and picket fence (with my 
> own quantum-optical desktop supercomputer and nanofactory in the den, of 
> course :-)  Does this dream seem laughable to the Silicon Valley computer 
> crowd?

Some of the biggest names in anarcho-capitalism belong to Easterners:
the Friedmans, Murray Rothbard, the Institute for Humane Studies, etc.
But you have a point; I detect a definite divergence in outlook between
the Western Extropians and us East-Coast types.  I see more open enthusiasm
for new ideas and trends in West Coast group, and more of a cynical,
subdued conservatism in the Easterners.  This may explain why I get into
so many arguments with Geoff Dale.

