Subject: [#94-12-393] SING: Post-singularity morals

Nancy Lebovitz wrote:
>Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.buffalo.edu> wrote: 
>>Anarcho-capitalists tend towards Nietzschian beliefs
>>(the eternal struggle is good and strengthens everyone -- similar
>>to the way market forces create efficiency), or towards nihilism.
>
>Speaking as an anarcho-capitalist, I believe that a combination
>of voluntary cooperation and competition works best.

As far as I understand the anarcho-capitalist position, cooperation will
develop since its more profitable in the long run (in the light of
Axelrods simulations of the iterated prisoners dilemma). Since we can
assume that most of the entities in a post-singularity world are rational,
they will cooperate since they will gain on it. 

Of course the problem is that some entities would profit much more with
cooperating with themselves than with lesser beings (what can an ant offer
a human?). This just reflects that morality appears to work best between
rather similar entities, and not between vastly different or powerful
entities. There will not only be a spectrum in complexity, there will 
also be a spectrum of alienness. 

Perhaps its wrong to think of interactions in terms of morality, maybe 
theology is more appropriate?


>>I think that if these memes are dominant post-Singularity, the Universe
>>will either (1.) be filled with unrelenting war between all manners of beings,
>>forever, or (2.) be the private preserve of a few gods.

Alternative 2 depends on whether such control is possible. If I break away
during the early Singularity with a very fast ship and move away as fast
as I can as far away I can, I could found a number of "civilizations" far
away from each other which could grow into sizeable intelligences before
they met the rest of posthumanity. If many transhumans did this, there
would always be a reservoir of divergent patterns. The whole thing is a
question of how much of spacetime is easy to reach, if its simple to reach
a lot of spacetime a god can quickly gain control over it, while if its a
hinder (for example since signal transmission is limited by the speed of
light) it would limit the power of the gods to at most a single solar
system, probably less. 

>I think that you're exagerating a bit here. There are quite a few
>species (like cockroaches) that people haven't managed to exterminate,
>alter, or get much use out of. 

There will doubtless be electronic cockroaches and mice. In fact, as Hans 
Moravec has pointed out, they might be a good source of new complexity. I 
could imagine "Complexity Gardners" who develop systems where new and 
unexpected complexity could develop. Combine the tenacity of a cockroach 
with some information gathering technology, and you might get the 
post-Singularity WebCrawler.


>Ubermen will no doubt do their own thinking about ethics, and not
>feel any obligation to be consistant with what *we're* doing, though
>the earlier Ubermen probably will start out with ethics rather like
>whatever they held in their pre-Ube condition. 

As Ettinger says in "Man into Superman", ethics has to evolve and change 
too. There is probably not a single, supreme ethics which works optimally 
in any sense. Instead each entity has to modify its ethical system 
according to its evolution and circumstances. 


>>The analogy of different post-Singularity species with biological niches
>>fails because of this.  There will be no room for less powerful beings
>>except as tools or pets of more powerful beings.  You will be either a
>>god, or a poodle.

A powerful being still has its limitations. Due to the speed of light, it
cannot be arbitrarily large to be cohesive. It either has to be spatially
finite or a diffuse and complex system of sub-units. A simple being can
fit in anywhere, and has no real problems with lightspeed since it can fit
on a single nanoprocessor or whatever is used. While the Jupiterbrains
think they run the solar system, smaller beings live in the Oort cloud, on
uncharted chips of rock or in unexplored parts of their operating system. 


>>The greatest diversity, the most complex interaction, the strangest and
>>most beautiful systems arise when competition is fair -- something
>
>Is competition fair in a rain forest? I'm not even sure what the
>question would mean when applied to nature.

Unfair competition leads to one side eventually winning. There was
probably "unfair competition" between the Amazonas forest and whatever
biotope preceded it. After the struggle was over, the jungle became
stable and evolved - possibly with pieces of the loosing biotope inside. 

Its quite possible that we will get a fractal hierarchy: unimaginable god
consisting of numerous semi-independent entities (since a monolithic
system has problems with delays, information handling etc), which in turn
consists of more entities, and so on to a low level where whole ecosystems
of small entities act as a part of the god - which they perhaps doesn't
notice. There may be many levels of metasystem transitions in a posthuman
being. 


>>anarcho-capitalists should appreciate.  This requires the most powerful
>>beings to allocate some resources to less powerful beings, without
>>seizing control of their evolution.  The only reason I can think of
>>for doing so is a moral imperative.
>
