I think Nietzche thought that the Ubermen would not care very much for 
each other, unless they choose to. As Crowley said, "Every man and woman 
is a star". They would move along and interact, but very seldom collide 
since there were so great distance between them.


>>I propose complexity as the fundamental value of such a Nietzschian variant.
>>Measuring complexity is, well, complex, but clearly a universe of
>>diverse creatures with diverse interactions would be more complex than a
>>monoculture.  Also, we get for free a dictum against violence:  destruction
>>increases entropy, which is the inverse of complexity.

This is my current ethics too. Its fairly easy to apply, although ones 
measure of complexity can change it quite a bit (do we care about current 
or future complexity?).

>If you still need food (rather than solar power), you have to destroy
>to live.

Solar power feeds on the increasing entropy of the poor sun. But if we 
didn't accept it, the sun would still shine.


>>So you see, I am not an environmentalist because watching sunsets gives me
>>warm squishy feelings.  It is the working out of a value system which I
>
